          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Religion and Culture</strong><br>\nReligion played a significant role during Hammurabi's reign. He portrayed himself as a god-like figure appointed by divine authority. This helped him maintain control over his vast empire. He was also a patron of the arts and is credited with enriching Babylonian culture, promoting the development of literature, science, and mathematics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Military Campaigns</strong><br>\nHammurabi's military campaigns were key to his success as a ruler. He often led his army into battle and was successful in his conquests, which included the defeat of the Elamites, the Subartu, and various other kingdoms in the region. These victories ensured Babylon's dominance in the region and the safety of its trade routes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hammurabi's Legacy</strong><br>\nHammurabi died around 1750 BCE, leaving behind a legacy that would influence the ancient world for centuries to come. His code formed the basis of legal systems in many civilizations that followed. His methods of ruling, the cultural advancements under his reign, and his vision for a unified state were significant contributions to the development of human civilization.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
